Final Findings
Peak taxi demand: 6 PM, with about 2.97 million trips.
Busiest day: Friday.
Lowest average taxi speed: around 12 PM, about 10.43 mph.
Highest delay ratio: 9 AM, with a ratio of 1.50, meaning travel took about 50% longer than the reference travel time.
Peak accident hour: 7 AM, with about 587,472 accident records.
Highest accident state: California in the accident dataset.
Junctions: accident records at junctions had a higher average severity than records without junctions.
Machine Learning: Linear Regression achieved approximately R² = 0.64, providing a useful baseline for travel-time prediction.
Logistic Regression: achieved 100% accuracy on its small test split, so this should be presented only as a baseline result, not as proof of general performance.
Recommendations
Manage morning traffic: Give special attention to the 8–10 AM period because the delay ratio is high.
Monitor midday congestion: The lowest average speed occurs around 12 PM, so congestion management should also consider midday traffic.
Improve peak-hour traffic management: The evening period has very high taxi demand, especially around 6 PM.
Focus on accident-prone periods: Traffic authorities can pay extra attention to the morning accident peak around 7 AM.
Improve junction management: Since junction-related accidents show higher average severity, junction safety and traffic control can be reviewed.
Use predictive models: The models can serve as a starting point for a future traffic congestion alert system.



